From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] schedsvc: scheduled tasks run

The Task Scheduler service stored the tasks registered through the Task
Scheduler 2.0 API (the XML files in system32\tasks) and never ran any of
them -- only the old AT jobs had a runner. Programs that keep themselves
up to date with a scheduled task never did: Microsoft Edge's
MicrosoftEdgeUpdateTaskMachineCore and ...UA tasks, Chromium-style
updaters. A browser installed for the whole machine stayed at the
version it was installed with. IRegisteredTask::Run was a stub, as were
the last run time and result, enabling and stopping.

The service now runs them (taskrun.c): the tasks of the system's own
accounts (SYSTEM, LOCAL SERVICE, NETWORK SERVICE, or no account given) --
what machine-wide installers register -- since the service runs as
SYSTEM; a particular user's task is that user's and is left alone. It
honours time triggers (once, or repeating every Interval for Duration),
daily and weekly calendar triggers and their repetitions, start
boundaries and end boundaries, boot triggers and the system accounts'
log-on triggers (at the service's start, after their Delay), registration
triggers, the task's and each trigger's Enabled, DisallowStartIfOnBatteries,
StartWhenAvailable (a run missed while the machine was off is made up),
MultipleInstancesPolicy and ExecutionTimeLimit. Exec actions run in
order, environment variables and $(Arg0).. expanded; the last one's exit
code is the result. Changes to the tasks folder are picked up as they
happen. The last run time, result and whether a task was disabled are
kept in HKLM\Software\Wine\Task Scheduler\State. SchRpcRun, SchRpcStop,
SchRpcGetLastRunInfo, SchRpcEnableTask and SchRpcGetTaskInfo's state
(running, queued, disabled) are answered.

The service starts with the system, as on Windows (StartType 2): the
first program to register a task no longer has to start it, which could
time out while the machine was still starting its services.

Not done: monthly calendars, event and session-state triggers, and a
particular user's tasks (they need running in that user's session).

Test: test/taskrun-gate.sh with test/taskrun-probe.c (mutant
SG_MUTANT_TASK_NO_RUNNER; with patch 1171).
